In Python, "constructors" refer to special methods used to initialize objects when a class is instantiated. The most common constructor is __init__.
Here’s a breakdown of how constructors work in Python and how you can use them effectively:
________________________________________
What Is a Constructor in Python?
•	Constructor is a method that gets called automatically when a new object of a class is created.
•	It sets up the initial state of the object by assigning values to its attributes.
________________________________________
Types of Constructors
1.	__init__ Method (Initializer)
o	Most commonly used constructor.
o	Automatically invoked after the object is created.
o	Syntax: 
o	class Person:
o	    def __init__(self, name, age):
o	        self.name = name
o	        self.age = age
o	When you create an object like p = Person("Alice", 30), __init__ sets name and age.
2.	__new__ Method (Object Creator)
o	Called before __init__.
o	Responsible for creating and returning a new instance.
o	Rarely overridden unless customizing object creation.
o	Syntax: 
o	class MyClass:
o	    def __new__(cls):
o	        instance = super(MyClass, cls).__new__(cls)
o	        return instance

 Key Concepts
•	Self parameter: Refers to the instance being created. Required in both __init__ and other instance methods.
•	Multiple constructors: Python doesn’t support method overloading directly, but you can simulate it using default arguments or *args and **kwargs.

Example with Default Values
class Car:
    def __init__(self, brand="Toyota", year=2020):
        self.brand = brand
        self.year = year

c1 = Car()
c2 = Car("Honda", 2022)

Why Use Constructors?
•	Ensure every object starts with a valid state.
•	Simplify object creation.
•	Encapsulate setup logic.

